name: interfaces and addresses
boss: Netcat
flavor: I listen where you cannot see.
brief: An interface is a way onto a network. ip is the modern tool for all of
brief: it -- addresses, links, routes, neighbours -- and ifconfig and route are
brief: the older ones you will still meet on machines that have not moved on.
brief:
brief: ip has a shape: ip OBJECT COMMAND. ip addr, ip link, ip route, ip neigh.
brief: Every one of them takes show, add and del.
---
q: you just booted this stick -- %show% every interface's addresses, with ip
a: ip addr
out: inet [0-9]
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: ip addr with no filter lists every interface and its addresses
---
q: piping this on -- %show% every interface's addresses one per line, with ip
a: ip -o addr
out: ^[0-9]+:.*inet
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: -o (oneline) puts each address on its own line for easy parsing
---
q: too many ipv6 lines -- %show% every interface's ipv4 addresses, with ip
a: ip -4 addr
why: live-hw
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: -4 filters ip's output down to ipv4 only
---
q: just plugged in the cable -- %show% every interface and its state, with ip
a: ip link
why: live-hw
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: ip link shows interfaces themselves, not the addresses on them
---
q: {bring the interface @iface@ up with ip|bring @iface@ up with ip|set @iface@ up with ip}
a: ip link set @iface@ up
why: live-hw
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: ip link set IFACE up/down toggles an interface's state
---
q: {bring @iface@ back down with ip|bring @iface@ down with ip|set @iface@ down with ip}
a: ip link set @iface@ down
why: live-hw
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: ip link set IFACE down is the mirror of ip link set IFACE up
---
q: {no dhcp -- give @iface@ the address @ip@ with a 24 bit netmask|assign @iface@ the address @ip@ with a /24 netmask|add the address @ip@/24 to @iface@}
a: ip addr add @ip@/24 dev @iface@
why: live-hw
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: addr add takes address/prefixlen, and dev to say where
---
q: {take @ip@/24 off @iface@ again|remove @ip@/24 from @iface@|delete @ip@/24, which you just added to @iface@}
a: ip addr del @ip@/24 dev @iface@
why: live-hw
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: ip addr del ADDR/PREFIX dev IFACE mirrors addr add
---
q: %show% the routing table, with ip
a: ip route
why: live-hw
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: ip route with no filter lists the whole table
---
q: is a gateway even configured -- %show% only the default route
a: ip route show default
why: live-hw
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: show default filters the route table to just the default route
---
q: {add a default route via @ip@|set @ip@ as the default route|make @ip@ the gateway for the default route}
a: ip route add default via @ip@
why: live-hw
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: route add default via sets where traffic with no other match goes
---
q: {delete the default route with ip|remove the default route with ip|take the default route out with ip}
a: ip route del default
why: live-hw
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: route del default is the mirror of add
---
q: who's been talking here -- %show% the arp neighbour table with ip
a: ip neigh
why: live-hw
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: ip neigh is ip's own view of the arp table
---
q: scripting -- %show% just the interface names, one per line, with ip
a: ip -o link | cut -d: -f2
out: ^ *lo *$
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: -o lines them up so cut can grab just the name field
---
q: %show% interfaces the old way
a: ifconfig
why: live-hw
ref: ifconfig
teach: ifconfig
hint: ifconfig with no arguments lists every interface, the pre-ip tool
---
q: %show% @iface@ specifically, the old way
a: ifconfig @iface@
why: live-hw
ref: ifconfig
teach: ifconfig
hint: name one interface and ifconfig shows only it
---
q: {set @iface@ up with @ip@, the old way|bring @iface@ up with @ip@, the old way|old-way command to give @iface@ the address @ip@ and bring it up}
a: ifconfig @iface@ @ip@ up
why: live-hw
ref: ifconfig
teach: ifconfig
hint: ifconfig combines address and up into one command, unlike ip's two steps
---
q: %show% the routing table the old way
a: route
why: live-hw
ref: route
teach: route
hint: route with no arguments is ifconfig's routing counterpart
---
q: dns might not even work yet -- %show% routes without names, using route
a: route -n
why: live-hw
ref: route
teach: route -n
hint: -n skips dns lookups on every address in the table
---
q: %show% route table entries with extended fields
a: route -e
why: live-hw
ref: route
teach: route -e
hint: -e adds the extra fields netstat-style output shows
---
q: see which macs this machine already knows about -- %show% the arp cache
a: arp
out: \) at .*\[
ref: arp
teach: arp
hint: arp with no arguments lists the whole cache
---
q: %show% the arp cache numerically, without resolving names
a: arp -n
why: live-net
ref: arp
teach: arp -n
hint: -n skips resolving hostnames for each entry
---
q: {mac changed, cache is stale -- delete the arp entry for @ip@|remove the arp entry for @ip@|drop @ip@ from the arp cache}
a: arp -d @ip@
why: live-hw
ref: arp
teach: arp -d
hint: -d removes one entry from the cache
---
q: {pin @ip@ to the mac 00:11:22:33:44:55 permanently, with arp|permanently map @ip@ to 00:11:22:33:44:55 with arp|add a static arp entry for @ip@ at 00:11:22:33:44:55}
a: arp -s @ip@ 00:11:22:33:44:55
why: live-hw
ref: arp
teach: arp -s
hint: -s pins an ip to a mac address by hand
---
q: %show% arp entries for @iface@ only
a: arp -i @iface@
why: live-hw
ref: arp
teach: arp -i
hint: -i filters the cache down to one interface
---
q: {publish an arp entry for @ip@ on @iface@, on another's behalf|answer arp requests for @ip@ on @iface@, on another's behalf|publish a proxy arp entry for @ip@ out of @iface@}
a: arp -D @iface@ -s @ip@
why: live-hw
ref: arp
teach: arp -D
hint: -D answers arp requests for an address on another host's behalf
---
setup: three probes, then stop
q: {is anyone using @ip@ -- ask at the link layer, with arping|check at the link layer whether @ip@ is in use, with arping|find out, below ip, whether @ip@ has an owner, with arping}
a: arping -c 3 @ip@
why: live-hw
ref: arping
teach: arping -c
hint: arp works below ip, so this finds hosts that will not answer a ping
---
setup: every arping here sends one probe, then stops
q: {send the arp probe for @ip@ out of @iface@|send the arp probe for @ip@ through @iface@|pick @iface@ as the interface the arp probe for @ip@ goes out of}
a: arping -I @iface@ -c 1 @ip@
why: live-hw
ref: arping
teach: arping -I
hint: -I picks which interface the probe goes out of
---
setup: every arping here sends one probe, then stops
q: {send a duplicate-address-detection probe for @ip@|probe for whether @ip@ is already a duplicate|send a duplicate-address-detection check for @ip@}
a: arping -D -c 1 @ip@
why: live-hw
ref: arping
teach: arping -D
hint: -D checks whether an address is already claimed before you use it
---
setup: every arping here sends one probe, then stops
q: {arping @ip@ using @ip2@ as the chosen source address|arping @ip@ with @ip2@ as the source address you choose|set @ip2@ as the source address when arping @ip@}
a: arping -s @ip2@ -c 1 @ip@
why: live-hw
ref: arping
teach: arping -s
hint: -s sets the source address the probe claims to be from
---
q: {stop arping @ip@ after 5 seconds whatever happens|cap arping @ip@ at 5 seconds no matter what|make arping @ip@ give up after 5 seconds}
a: arping -w 5 @ip@
why: live-hw
ref: arping
teach: arping -w
hint: -w caps the total time arping will run
---
q: {work out the network settings for the address @ip@/24|derive the network settings for @ip@/24|break down @ip@/24 into its network settings}
a: ipcalc @ip@/24
cmp: ref
ref: ipcalc
teach: ipcalc
hint: ipcalc with no flags prints the whole network breakdown
---
q: %show% just the broadcast address for @ip@/24, with ipcalc
a: ipcalc -b @ip@/24
cmp: ref
ref: ipcalc
teach: ipcalc -b
hint: -b isolates just the broadcast address
---
q: %show% just the network address for @ip@/24, with ipcalc
a: ipcalc -n @ip@/24
cmp: ref
ref: ipcalc
teach: ipcalc -n
hint: -n isolates just the network address
---
q: %show% just the netmask for @ip@/24, with ipcalc
a: ipcalc -m @ip@/24
cmp: ref
ref: ipcalc
teach: ipcalc -m
hint: -m isolates just the netmask
---
q: %show% @ip@'s prefix length for netmask 255.255.255.0, with ipcalc
a: ipcalc -p @ip@ 255.255.255.0
cmp: ref
ref: ipcalc
teach: ipcalc -p
hint: -p converts a dotted netmask into its prefix length
